#use(std/code)

#section{Hanging blocks (::)}::

#code{::} at the end of a call line folds the #strong{rest of the enclosing
block} — same indentation level, until dedent or end of file — into the
call's body, without indenting it. The title of this very page uses it:
#code|{#section{Hanging blocks (::)}::}| captures the rest of the file.

#code(lang: "plain")
	#blockquote
		intro
		#emph::
		alpha
		#strong::
		beta

Live (the #code{::}s capture the rest of the blockquote's indented block; the
later #code{::} nests inside the earlier one):

#blockquote
	intro
	#emph::
	alpha
	#strong::
	beta

Corner cases:

#list
	#item: Nothing may follow #code{::} on the same line — that is a reader error.
	#item: #code{::} vs a single #code{:} at end of line: both give a call with an inline body an #emph{additional} block body, but #code{::} folds the un-indented rest of the enclosing block (hanging), whereas #code{:} takes the block indented one level deeper below it. #code|{#section{T}::}| and #code|{#section{T}:}| produce the same call shape from differently-laid-out source.
	#item: It is a pure reader-level transform; evaluation sees an ordinary body.
	#item: Recursive by construction: a later #code{::} at the same level captures the remainder of the block it now lives in, so it nests inside the earlier one. There is no flat "until the next same-name #code{::}" variant.
	#item: At top level, #code{::} means the rest of the file — the idiomatic page shape is #code|{#section{Title}::}| at the top.
	#item: #code{::} in plain prose is just text; only a call directly followed by #code{::} is special. See #code{std}::vector — rendered literally.
